Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 JUN 1},
Abstract = {Although political trust is commonly understood as a relation between
   subject (truster) and object (trustee), research almost exclusively
   focuses on citizens. The inherent agency of politicians has been
   overlooked. This article sets three aims: (1) to develop a codebook to
   analyse trust in texts and speeches by politicians, (2) to describe the
   use of political trust in parliamentary debates, and (3) to understand
   how MPs employ political trust in these debates. It studies annual
   state-of-the-nation debates in four European (high-trust and low-trust;
   majoritarian and proportional) democracies between 1998 and 2018.
   Generally, political trust receives little attention in these debates.
   Over time, politicians' attention for political trust coincides with
   economic hardship and rising radical-right populism. Moreover,
   politicians discuss trust predominantly for strategic purposes
   (discrediting others; praising themselves), rather than as a substantive
   concern. This may contribute to a politicisation of political distrust
   that may harm legitimacy in the long run.},
